\description{
This page documents the DIMS functions and the \code{purityD} class and
methods retained for compatibility. Shared functions used by the LC-MS
workflows remain supported. The \code{purityD} method for
\code{updatePeaks()} is also deprecated.
}
\details{
With hierarchical clustering, matching peaks stay together. The call stops
with an error if more than \code{blockSize} nearby peaks cannot form one group or be
separated without splitting matching peaks.
}
\section{DIMS retirement}{

The DIMS workflow is deprecated and will receive no further bug fixes or
feature development. There is no direct replacement in msPurity. Calls
remain functional during deprecation; the interfaces will become defunct
and be removed in subsequent release cycles.
}

\section{get_additional_mzml_meta}{

Extract the filter strings 'accession MS:1000512' from an mzML file. Called header in thermo software.
Enables quick access to various information regarding each scan

\strong{Value:} dataframe of meta info
}

\section{purityD}{

The class used to assess anticipated purity from a DI-MS run

\strong{Value:} purityD object
}

\section{getP}{

output peak list for a purityD object

\strong{Value:} peaks
}

\section{show (purityD method)}{

Show method for purityD object

\strong{Value:} a print statement of regarding object
}

\section{initialize (purityD method)}{

The class used to predict purity from an DI-MS dataset.

\strong{Value:} purityD object. The files and their sample information are also kept as an \code{MsExperiment} in
the experiment slot, and the averaged peak lists of later steps as \code{Spectra} in the avSpectra slot
(see \code{\link{averagedPeaks}})
}

\section{averageSpectra}{

If \code{cores} is omitted, the call uses \code{Object@cores}
without changing that stored setting.

Uses a purityD object with references to multiple MS files. For each file:
Averages multiple scans together,
see averageSpectraSingle for more information

\strong{Value:} purityD object with averaged spectra
}

\section{averageSpectraSingle}{

Averages multiple scans of mass spectrometry data together.
Each scan consisting of a minimum of intensity and mz values.

Works for either mzML or a .csv file consisting of mz, i, scanid,
(optional: noise, backgroun, snr)

Signal-to-noise (SNR) can be calculated a number of ways. Default is to
calculate the SN for every scan as the
"Intensity of peak / the median intensity of the scan".

Alternatively if using a .csv file as input (and assigning the csvFile parameter to TRUE), a precalculated SNR can be one of the
columns. The precalculated SNR can then be chosen by using the option 'precalc' for the parameter snMethod

The function will work for both LC-MS or DI-MS datasets.

\strong{Value:} dataframe of the median mz, intensity, signal-to-noise ratio.
}

\section{groupPeaks}{

If \code{cores} is omitted, the call uses \code{Object@cores}
without changing that stored setting.

Uses a purityD object to group all the peaklists in the 'avPeaks$processing' slot

\strong{Value:} data.frame of peaklists grouped together by mz
}

\section{groupPeaksEx}{

Group a list of dataframes by their m/z values

\strong{Value:} data.frame of peaklists grouped together by mz
}

\section{dimsPredictPurity}{

Assess the precursor purity of anticpated MS/MS spectra.
i.e. it 'predicts' the precursor purity of the DI-MS peaks for a future MS/MS run.

\strong{Value:} purityD object with predicted purity of peaks
}

\section{dimsPredictPuritySingle}{

Given a an DI-MS dataset (either mzML or .csv file) calculate the predicted
purity for a vector of mz values.

Calculated at a given offset e.g. for 0.5 +/- Da the minOffset would be 0.5
and the maxOffset of 0.5.

A ppm tolerance is used to find the target mz value in each scan.

\strong{Value:} a dataframe of the target mz values and the predicted purity score
}

\section{Getfiles}{

Takes in a folder path and outputs the a data frame structure for purityD.
Function modified from mzmatch.

\strong{Value:} dataframe of files
}

\section{filterp}{

Uses a purityD object remove peaks from either (or both) samples and
blanks that are either below an intensity threshold
or greater than a Relative Standard Deviation (RSD) threshold

\strong{Value:} purityD object
}

\section{updateObject (purityD method)}{

Objects saved before msPurity stored the averaged DIMS peak lists in a
\code{Spectra} object lack the \code{avSpectra} and \code{experiment}
slots. \code{updateObject()} builds them from \code{avPeaks} and
\code{fileList}. Every purityD method updates its input this way.

\strong{Value:} An updated purityD object.
}

\section{averagedPeaks}{

The averaged peak list of each file, as the \code{avPeaks} list (a list
with elements \code{orig} and \code{processed}, each with one data frame
per file; \code{legacy = TRUE}, the default) or as a \code{Spectra}
object with one spectrum per file and stage, with the other columns as
peak variables (\code{legacy = FALSE}). The legacy \code{avPeaks} slot is
only filled while \code{options(msPurity.legacySlots = TRUE)}, the
default for this release.

\strong{Value:} A list or a \code{Spectra} object.
}

\section{subtract}{

Uses a purityD object with references to multiple MS files. Subtract blank peaks from the sample peaks
see subtractMZ for more information

\strong{Value:} purityD object with averaged spectra
}

\section{subtractMZ}{

This function is intended for blank subtraction of mz values from two peaklists. It takes in 2 vectors of mz values and 2
coresponding vectors of Intensity values.

The second mz values are subtracted from the first set within an MZ tolerance.

However, if the mz match but the intensity is above a defined threshold then they are not subtracted

\strong{Value:} a vector of the remaining mz values
}

\section{writeOut}{

Writes processed peak lists to CSV files, and also writes the original
peak lists when \code{original = TRUE}.

\strong{Value:} purityD object
}

